(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A man with a pustular beard eruption has hairs that are firmly anchored and do not pull out easily. Which test does the lecture suggest to help separate this from tinea barbae?","choices":["Patch testing","Serology for dermatophyte antibodies","Bacterial culture","Wood lamp examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — used to rule out bacterial folliculitis. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"The same mother asks whether the lesion will need surgery. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will fade within her first year without any treatment","It will regress over three to six months after a period of stability","Most involute on their own over several years","It will grow with her throughout life and will not involute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — complete involution in fifty per cent by age five, seventy per cent by seven and ninety per cent by nine, so serial observation is often all that is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How is naevus spilus managed?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and total spine at presentation","Observation with periodic clinical evaluation, plus sun protection counselling","Excision with negative margins at the time it is first identified clinically","Cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal if preferred"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What distinguishes solar lentigo from lentigo maligna?","choices":["Solar lentigo appears in early childhood; lentigo maligna appears only after the age of seventy","Solar lentigo appears on covered skin; lentigo maligna on exposed skin","Solar lentigo has uniform pigmentation; lentigo maligna is asymmetric with irregular border and colour","Solar lentigo is raised and rough; lentigo maligna is flat and smooth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and dermoscopy is essential — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for seborrhoeic dermatitis?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","Oral doxycycline","Topical antifungals such as ketoconazole, with steroids used early to reduce inflammation","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone, continued long term as monotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Scalp disease uses ketoconazole or selenium sulfide shampoo; facial disease uses ketoconazole cream or lotion. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How should laboratory tests and imaging be sequenced in suspected necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["They should not delay surgical intervention, because this is a surgical emergency with high mortality","They should be completed and reviewed before surgery, because the findings determine the incision","They should replace surgery where imaging is negative, because operating carries its own risk","They should be repeated at forty-eight hours, because early results are usually uninformative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood count, chemistry, arterial blood gas, urinalysis and cultures are all sent. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has diffuse thinning over the central and parietal scalp with her frontal hairline preserved. Which is the most appropriate treatment consideration?","choices":["Antifungal shampoo","Oral antiandrogens such as combined oral contraceptives may be considered, since treatment options are limited","Oral finasteride prescribed as first-line therapy for female-pattern hair loss at any age after puberty","Intralesional corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — female-pattern hair loss has fewer options, and finasteride is not indicated in women. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"What is a telangiectasia?","choices":["An acquired proliferation of capillaries and venules forming a deep red papule under five millimetres","A rapidly growing vascular papule that arises in response to trauma or hormonal change","A permanently dilated capillary under one millimetre, blanchable","A congenital dilation of dermal capillaries through the full depth of the dermis, present at birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an acquired vascular lesion, single or in groups, with a central punctum. It may be primary or secondary and is associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is pyoderma gangrenosum, despite its name?","choices":["A vasculitis of medium-sized vessels with immune complex deposition","A malignant ulcerating tumour of keratinocyte origin","A neutrophilic dermatosis that is not infectious","A deep bacterial infection requiring prolonged antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dysregulated innate immune activation with neutrophil recruitment causes the tissue destruction. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 52-year-old's melanoma is reported at 0.6 mm Breslow thickness with no adverse features. Which re-excision margin applies?","choices":["A 1 to 2 cm margin","A 3 cm margin","A 1 cm margin","A 0.5 to 1 cm margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lesions under 1 mm take a 1 cm margin. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Sensitivity is specifically related to fewer of which type of erroneous result?","choices":["Fewer false-positives","Fewer true-positives","Fewer true-negatives","Fewer false-negatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states sensitivity is related to fewer false-negatives, and notes explicitly that sensitivity does not address false-positives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has mild pubic itching, slate-grey to bluish irregular macules about 1 cm across on the lower abdomen, and papules around the umbilicus. Which additional step is most appropriate?","choices":["Screen for diabetes, since the intertriginous distribution suggests an underlying metabolic cause","Screen for immunosuppression, since the massive mite burden indicates impaired immunity","Screen for coeliac disease, since the eruption is associated with gluten sensitivity","Screen for concurrent sexually transmitted infection, since pubic lice often coexist with one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the macules are maculae caerulae, representing haemorrhage at feeding sites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which intervention is described as the single most evidence-supported for photoaging?","choices":["Chemical peeling with glycolic or trichloroacetic acid","Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen at SPF 30 or above","Ablative laser resurfacing performed every few years","Daily topical vitamin C at 10 to 20% concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, for both preventing and slowing progression, and beginning in childhood gives maximal lifetime benefit. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise behavioural counselling?","choices":["As a task that is best delegated to non-clinical support staff within the practice","As unnecessary when a guideline exists","As effective only for tobacco cessation","As one of the most important skills for helping patients make behavioural changes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states changing behaviours is difficult and that behavioural counselling is one of the most important skills for supporting it. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient has just had a basal cell carcinoma excised and asks whether that is the end of it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Recurrence at the same site is near-certain, so a further excision should be booked now","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so at least annual full-skin examination is mandatory","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in under 1% of patients, so no further surveillance is needed","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so examination every five years is sufficient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives lifelong surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Who should receive recombinant zoster vaccine, and how many doses?","choices":["One dose for immunocompetent adults aged 65 and over, and one dose for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","Two doses only for adults who have never had chickenpox, and no doses at all for anyone with a history of varicella or of prior zoster","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 19 and over, and none for adults who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over, and two doses for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — standard interval two to six months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A patient asks why her surgical scar has become stronger over the past year. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["Progressive proliferation of endothelial cells within the scar","Progressive accumulation of keratin beneath the scar surface","Progressive replacement of collagen by adipose tissue","Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibers as the scar matures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the remodeling phase of wound healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 22-year-old wrestler has a single round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous dry scaly plaque on his forearm. The centre has begun to clear, leaving a ring. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Fixed drug eruption","Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Discoid lupus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — progressive central clearing gives the annular \"ringworm\" outline. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Dermoscopy is performed on that patient's leg lesion. Which finding would support the diagnosis?","choices":["An epithelial collarette at the base of the lesion","A central keratin-filled crater within a smooth dome","A peripheral pigment network with a central white mass","A moth-eaten border with uniform pigment throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the white centre corresponds to the fibrous core. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Where should a patient with necrotizing fasciitis be admitted?","choices":["An ambulatory unit for daily dressing changes and review","A surgical intensive care unit, ideally a burn or trauma centre","A general medical ward with hourly observations","A dermatology inpatient unit for wound management"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a team approach with consultations is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"What are the four phases of wound healing, in order?","choices":["Hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodeling","Inflammation, hemostasis, remodeling, proliferation","Proliferation, hemostasis, inflammation, remodeling","Hemostasis, proliferation, inflammation, remodeling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — abnormal healing is loss of control over this balance. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What determines management of a congenital melanocytic naevus?","choices":["Pigment intensity alone, with darker lesions excised and lighter ones observed","Melanoma risk together with cosmetic and functional considerations, removing as much as possible while preserving function","Lesion size alone, with every lesion over 5 mm excised regardless of its site or of graft availability at all","Patient age alone, with all lesions excised before the child reaches school age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — observation may be the better option where there is little skin for a graft site. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which history elements does the deck ask you to elicit?","choices":["Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy","Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does the lecture caution against dismissing vitiligo as cosmetic?","choices":["It always progresses to universal depigmentation if it is left untreated","It affects patients psychologically and socially, through low self-esteem and poor body image","It carries a high risk of transformation into melanoma within the depigmented areas","It indicates an underlying internal malignancy in a substantial minority of patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — psychological intervention is named as part of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which age group is most affected by head lice, and how is it usually transmitted?","choices":["Infants under one year, primarily by contact with the bedding of an infested carer","Children between three and twelve years, primarily by direct head-to-head contact","Adults over forty years, primarily by sexual contact and shared towels or bedding","Homeless adults, primarily by contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Indirect spread through combs, brushes, bedding and headgear is less common. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which systemic therapy has the strongest current evidence in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given for the first week","Prophylactic antibiotics covering skin and gut flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with intravenous immunoglobulin and etanercept as adjunct options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How is scaling described?","choices":["Accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white","A dried crust of serum","A scratch or abrasion in which the top layer of the skin is worn away by repeated rubbing or friction","Thickening and hardening of the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man in Oregon was bitten in his basement in August. He felt nothing at the time, then noticed hardness and tingling within half an hour, and a blister formed the next day. Which spider is most likely responsible?","choices":["The brown recluse spider","The black widow spider","A tarantula that had been kept as a pet","The hobo spider"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the Pacific Northwest, a painless bite, induration and paraesthesia within thirty minutes, vesicles by thirty-six hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How is an id reaction treated?","choices":["With a potent topical corticosteroid to the distant site","With oral antifungal directed at the distant site","With oral antihistamines alone until it settles","It resolves when the primary dermatophyte infection is treated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treating the distant eruption itself is not the answer. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had furuncles recur repeatedly over two years. He has a body mass index of 36 and a haemoglobin A1c of 8.4%. Which three factors should be addressed?","choices":["Smoking, hot weather and obstruction of the apocrine ducts","Impaired lymphatic drainage, tinea pedis and previous impetigo","Occupational water immersion, chemical contact and nail biting","Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those are the three predisposing factors named for recurrent furunculosis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What causes herpes zoster, and what raises the risk?","choices":["Reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus; risk rises with age and impaired cell-mediated immunity","A new infection with varicella-zoster virus caught from an infected contact; risk rises with crowding and poor hygiene","Reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in a sensory ganglion; risk rises with ultraviolet exposure and febrile illness","An autoimmune response to a previous varicella infection; risk rises with atopy and connective-tissue disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the virus stays latent in cranial-nerve or dorsal-root ganglia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How is mild perioral dermatitis treated once the corticosteroid is stopped?","choices":["A higher-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily","Topical ketoconazole","Oral prednisone","Topical metronidazole, erythromycin, pimecrolimus or azelaic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with oral tetracycline or doxycycline for more extensive or persistent disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What defines stage IV melanoma in the staging overview?","choices":["T4b, N3a/b/c, with M0 — the deepest primary with extensive nodal disease but no distant spread","Any T with N0 and M0 — a thick primary with no nodal or distant disease","Tis with any N and any M — in situ disease with any degree of spread","Any T, any N, with M1 — that is, distant metastasis present whatever the primary or nodal status"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — M1 alone determines it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A fluid-filled lesion greater than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A pustule","A nodule","A bulla","A vesicle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a vesicle is the same lesion up to one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman is booked for intense pulsed light treatment of her freckles. What must she be told to expect?","choices":["The lesions can relapse, so this is not necessarily permanent","The result is permanent once a full course has been completed","Her freckles will darken before they fade, over about six weeks","She will need lifelong monthly maintenance treatments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck says explicitly that lesions can relapse after light or laser treatment. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient with an advanced basal cell carcinoma is no longer a surgical candidate. Which systemic option does the deck give?","choices":["A hedgehog pathway inhibitor","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel","Romidepsin or vorinostat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reserved for advanced or metastatic disease or extensive tumour burden. Vismodegib or sonidegib. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with numerous solar lentigines asks what her lesions mean for her health. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions themselves are benign, but they mark sun damage that is also associated with actinic keratosis and skin cancers","The lesions are premalignant and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a melanoma","The lesions indicate an underlying internal malignancy and warrant an urgent systemic screening of the whole body","The lesions will fade over the coming winter if she avoids further exposure to the sun"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the association includes squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has been prescribed permethrin for scabies. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Apply it overnight to the entire skin surface with attention to creases, and repeat the application one week later","Apply it overnight to the affected areas only, and no repeat application will be needed afterwards","Wash the skin vigorously with a strong soap before each application to remove the mites","Expect all itching to have stopped within three days, and return if any remains after that"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the second application is the point most often missed. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"When can cellulitis be managed without any diagnostic workup?","choices":["Only after blood cultures have returned negative and inflammatory markers are normal","Limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs and no risk factor for serious illness","Any case in which the patient is afebrile, regardless of the extent of the affected area","Any case occurring in a patient under fifty years of age with no history of diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serious infections warrant blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"In which patients might scabies excoriations be seen along the head and neck?","choices":["Patients who have already been treated once","Infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised","Adolescents and young adults in shared housing","Pregnant patients in their third trimester"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — healthy adults have head and neck sparing. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman admitted after a hip fracture has a heel wound with full-thickness skin loss and yellow adipose tissue clearly visible in the base. No tendon or bone can be seen. Which stage is this?","choices":["Stage 3","Stage 4","Stage 2","Unstageable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — full thickness with fat visible, and nothing deeper exposed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who drives a lorry presents with sudden pain and swelling over his sacrum. There is a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant swelling in the gluteal cleft with a hair protruding from a small opening. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epidermoid cyst","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Pilonidal abscess","Stage 2 pressure injury"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the natal cleft, the sinus opening and the protruding hair. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman asks how a mole she was born with differs from the ones that appeared later. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Congenital naevi arise from keratinocytes in the basal layer; acquired naevi arise from dermal fibroblasts within the skin","Congenital naevi arise from hair follicle precursors; acquired naevi arise from vascular endothelium in the dermis","Congenital naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles; acquired naevi arise from junctional melanocytes","Congenital naevi arise from junctional melanocytes in the epidermis; acquired naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursor cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic naevi are the group with atypical architectural and cytologic features. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with pityriasis rosea asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It requires a six-week course of oral antibiotics","It will persist indefinitely without phototherapy","It is highly contagious by direct skin contact, so he should avoid close contact with others until every last lesion has faded","It is self-limited and typically fades over 4 to 6 weeks, though darker skin may show post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation for several months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"Which three genera account for the majority of dermatophyte infections?","choices":["Microsporum, Malassezia, Epidermophyton","Trichophyton, Aspergillus, Epidermophyton","Microsporum, Trichophyton, Epidermophyton","Candida, Malassezia, Trichophyton"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and infections are then classified by body location. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is a concern in a drained furuncle?","choices":["Defer drainage until the culture result becomes available","Culture the material obtained at incision and drainage","Start linezolid empirically before any material is obtained","Swab the nasal vestibule of every household contact first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and give endocarditis prophylaxis to at-risk patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which class is the typical first-line topical therapy for comedonal and inflammatory acne?","choices":["Topical retinoids, which are comedolytic and normalise hyperkeratinisation","Topical antimicrobials, which act against Cutibacterium acnes in the follicle","Topical dapsone gel, which is anti-inflammatory by an unknown mechanism","Topical azelaic acid, which is antimicrobial, comedolytic and anti-inflammatory"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — adapalene, tazarotene and tretinoin, generally the most effective for mild disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with Crohn's disease has biopsy-supported pyoderma gangrenosum that continues to enlarge on high-dose prednisone. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously","Surgical debridement of the ulcer bed followed by grafting","Omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Topical high-potency corticosteroid applied to the ulcer base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is approved for pyoderma gangrenosum in inflammatory bowel disease and is the biologic of choice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic red-brown patches on the inner thighs and between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp they fluoresce coral-red. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea cruris","Cutaneous candidiasis","Inverse psoriasis","Erythrasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — coral-red fluorescence in intertriginous skin, with diabetics at high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Specificity is related to fewer of which type of erroneous result, and is silent about which other?","choices":["Fewer false-negatives; silent about false-positives","Fewer false-positives; silent about false-negatives","Fewer true-positives; silent about true-negatives","Fewer false-positives; silent about true-positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Specificity is related to fewer false-positives, and the lecture notes that specificity does not address false-negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"You are about to prescribe a systemic antifungal for an 8-year-old with confirmed tinea capitis. Which safety step does the lecture identify before the first dose?","choices":["Review drug interactions and renal disease, and obtain a baseline creatinine in every patient regardless of the agent chosen","Review drug interactions and cardiac disease, and obtain a baseline electrocardiogram in every patient before the first dose","No safety step is needed, because oral antifungals used for scalp disease carry no clinically important interactions","Review drug interactions and hepatic disease, and obtain baseline liver tests when indicated by the agent, its labeling, and patient risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — baseline testing is indication-driven, not automatic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What caveat does the deck attach to the 6 mm diameter criterion?","choices":["Smaller lesions can be melanoma","Larger lesions are never melanoma","The threshold applies only to lesions on the trunk","The threshold applies only to patients over 50"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the threshold is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which pair of barriers to adherence does the lecture list?","choices":["Cultural or religious beliefs about treatment, and fear of side effects or mistrust of providers","High self-efficacy and strong family support","Short travel distance and abundant appointment availability","Excess follow-up appointments and over-explanation of the possible side effects of treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both appear on the lecture's list of adherence barriers. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking","No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility","Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient's scabies has not cleared after individual therapy. Which regimen is described?","choices":["A mid-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily for a month","Ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses, with topical permethrin every three days to weekly","A third overnight application of topical permethrin given one week after the second one","Oral albendazole for three days, repeated if there is no response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same regimen used for hyperkeratotic or immunosuppressed disease. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What drives the commonest, insulin-resistant form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes within the affected intertriginous skin","Chronic ultraviolet exposure driving proliferation of the basal layer melanocytes and increased melanin production","Immunoglobulin A deposition in the dermal papillae activating neutrophils","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Malignant, drug-induced and endocrine forms make up the rest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A neonate develops blisters over the hands and feet after routine handling and nappy changes. There is no fever and the mother is well. Family history includes a father with similar childhood blistering that healed without scars. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Junctional epidermolysis bullosa of the Herlitz subtype","Bullous pemphigoid of the neonatal period","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — localised to palms and soles, healing without scarring, and autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with a rash that shows discrete circumscribed papules and, in the older areas, crusting and scaling. Which conclusion follows from the crusting?","choices":["The infection is bacterial in origin","The lesions are resolving spontaneously","The subcutaneous tissue is involved","The epidermis has been affected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — crust and scale indicate epidermal involvement, which narrows the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with extensive vitiligo says the condition troubles him socially far more than physically. Which aspect of care does the deck single out alongside repigmentation?","choices":["Annual skin cancer surveillance for every patient","Immediate referral for surgical grafting in all patients","Psychological intervention, alongside cosmetic and non-traditional therapies","Screening the whole family for autoimmune thyroid disease at diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is listed as part of management rather than as an afterthought. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which exposure most commonly precipitates perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Repeated shaving of the beard area with a blade","Topical corticosteroid applied to the face","Prolonged immersion of the hands in water","Occlusion of the skin under a plaster cast"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — continued exposure perpetuates it, so the steroid must be stopped. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"}]};
